Situation:
The division sustainment brigade is conducting ground resupply in a contested corridor. Multiple supply depots hold limited stocks of critical classes of supply (e.g., ammunition and fuel). Several forward maneuver and support units are dispersed across the sector with varying urgency and access constraints. Ground lines of communication include a main valley highway under intermittent observation, a mountain pass subject to route-denial threats, and secondary farm roads that are slower but less conspicuous. Convoys require armed escort and route-clearance support; air resupply is constrained by weather and air defense threats. Enemy activity, damaged infrastructure, and choke points compress delivery windows and increase movement risk.

Mission:
Deliver required classes of supply from available depots to designated forward units within the current operational period while minimizing total operational cost, to include transportation cost, shortage penalties for unmet demand, vehicle deployment cost, and fixed route security risk costs.

Execution/Concept:
- Depots will release supplies subject to on-hand stock limitations; no location may dispatch more than it holds for each class of supply during this period.
- Unit demands must be met from any combination of depots; controlled shortfalls may be accepted only where operationally necessary and will incur shortage penalties.
- Vehicle assignments will respect payload capacity; the total volumetric load shipped on any depot-to-unit movement cannot exceed the product of vehicle capacity and the number of vehicles allocated to that movement.
- Route activation is gated: vehicles can only be dispatched on routes that are formally activated for the period. Activating any route (i.e., sending one or more vehicles on a given depot-to-unit corridor) incurs a fixed Risk Cost representing the security overhead for escorts, route clearance, and overwatch—assessed once per activated route regardless of the number of vehicles using it.
- The vehicle fleet is limited for the period; total vehicles assigned across all routes cannot exceed the available fleet. Each vehicle deployed carries an operating cost.
- Transportation costs vary by corridor and reflect distance, terrain, and expected delays. Risk Costs are fixed per activated route. Shortage penalties reflect operational impact of unmet demand. Supply class volumes determine how many vehicles are required per quantity delivered.
- The movement control team will sequence convoys to minimize exposure on threatened approaches, consolidate deliveries where feasible to avoid unnecessary route activations, and prioritize high-impact demands. Alternate corridors may be used when risk or capacity constraints preclude main-route movements.

